REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w04","title":"Spoken English: tempo, pauses, talks and phone calls","summary":"What matters most when you speak English to the world: something to say, politeness, a slow tempo and a pause after every phrase, clause and sentence. Then how to plan a short talk, how to present it, and the set phrases of telephone English.","n":4,"lectures":[{"code":"4.1","title":"Spoken english preliminaries","video":"_mD9iGMD73g","seconds":1889},{"code":"4.2","title":"Speak slowly","video":"d2iAbGoxazg","seconds":1813},{"code":"4.3","title":"Phrasal pause","video":"tGch4v-bKsU","seconds":1644},{"code":"4.4","title":"Do you have something To say?","video":"XfbkqslFsTA","seconds":1851},{"code":"4.5","title":"Effective presentations","video":"M0jkrPkUbTg","seconds":2071},{"code":"4.6","title":"Telephone english","video":"VSwUcHGAMcE","seconds":1464}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"english-for-the-world-what-matters-most","level":2,"title":"English for the world: what matters most"},{"id":"have-something-to-say","level":3,"title":"Have something to say"},{"id":"politeness","level":3,"title":"Politeness"},{"id":"slow-tempo","level":3,"title":"Slow tempo"},{"id":"word-stress-varies","level":3,"title":"Word stress varies"},{"id":"long-and-short-vowels","level":3,"title":"Long and short vowels"},{"id":"good-listening-habits","level":3,"title":"Good listening habits"},{"id":"books-a-dictionary-and-a-daily-drill","level":3,"title":"Books, a dictionary and a daily drill"},{"id":"speak-slowly","level":2,"title":"Speak slowly"},{"id":"slow-speech-is-polite-speech","level":3,"title":"Slow speech is polite speech"},{"id":"how-to-learn-to-speak-slowly","level":3,"title":"How to learn to speak slowly"},{"id":"phrasal-pause","level":2,"title":"Phrasal pause"},{"id":"where-to-pause","level":3,"title":"Where to pause"},{"id":"do-you-have-something-to-say","level":2,"title":"Do you have something to say?"},{"id":"prepare","level":3,"title":"Prepare"},{"id":"quantify-and-find-the-data","level":3,"title":"Quantify, and find the data"},{"id":"make-it-interesting-and-relevant","level":3,"title":"Make it interesting and relevant"},{"id":"effective-presentations","level":2,"title":"Effective presentations"},{"id":"stage-manners","level":3,"title":"Stage manners"},{"id":"relax-and-smile","level":3,"title":"Relax and smile"},{"id":"posture-hands-and-eyes","level":3,"title":"Posture, hands and eyes"},{"id":"voice","level":3,"title":"Voice"},{"id":"slides","level":3,"title":"Slides"},{"id":"telephone-english","level":2,"title":"Telephone English"},{"id":"making-a-call","level":3,"title":"Making a call"},{"id":"taking-a-call","level":3,"title":"Taking a call"},{"id":"asking-to-wait","level":3,"title":"Asking to wait"},{"id":"a-second-call-and-a-conference-call","level":3,"title":"A second call and a conference call"},{"id":"phone-problems","level":3,"title":"Phone problems"},{"id":"ending-a-call","level":3,"title":"Ending a call"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What to learn first</strong> (4.1): English is the world’s language, spoken in every accent, so aim at the <strong>global standard</strong> that everyone can follow. First <strong>have something to say</strong>, then be <strong>polite</strong>, then speak <strong>slowly</strong>. Learn <strong>word stress</strong> (it changes from word to word) and <strong>long and short vowels</strong>. Good speaking grows from <strong>good listening</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Speak slowly</strong> (4.2): English is a <strong>slow-tempo</strong> language. Slow speech is clear and <strong>polite</strong>, and it gives you time to think; rapid speech merges and drops sounds. Faster than <strong>3 or 4 syllables a second</strong>, or <strong>100 words a minute</strong>, is rapid.</li>\n<li><strong>Phrasal pause</strong> (4.3): pause after every <strong>phrase</strong>, <strong>clause</strong> and <strong>sentence</strong> (# short, ## long). Pause after greetings, words of address, every 2 or 3 digits, each level of an address, words like <em>so, but, therefore</em>, and each item in a list. <strong>Pause is the punctuation of speech.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Something to say</strong> (4.4): great speakers are remembered for <strong>what</strong> they said. A two-minute talk on a civic problem says <strong>where, what, how much</strong> (with numbers) and <strong>its effects</strong>. Make it <strong>interesting</strong> and <strong>relevant</strong>, and rehearse to fit the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Effective presentations</strong> (4.5): every conversation is a <strong>performance</strong>. Be well turned out and relaxed but alert; greet with a smile; use your hands with meaning; keep <strong>eye contact</strong>; <strong>modulate</strong> your voice; keep slides light. <strong>Creativity + language + data.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Telephone English</strong> (4.6): you <strong>make</strong> or <strong>take</strong> a call, formal or informal, with set phrases for every step, from <em>May I speak to Padmini, please?</em> to <em>I need to hang up now</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"english-for-the-world-what-matters-most\">English for the world: what matters most</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.1 opens the module on spoken English. The teacher is Professor Shreesh Chaudhary (Humanities and Social Sciences, IIT Madras). Two questions: what must we remember while learning to speak <strong>global standard English</strong>, and which parts of spoken English come first? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Why spoken English?” The slide answers: <strong>global language</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=99s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:39</a>). Since about 1945 English has been the language of the world. In South Asia, East Africa, the West Indies and elsewhere it is used in many domains, from home to office (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). All sorts of people speak it, so it comes in <strong>all sorts of accents</strong>. (An <em>accent</em> is a way of pronouncing a language that is typical of a place or a group.) Unless we speak the global standard, many listeners will not understand us, and we will not understand them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Global standard English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has a “mind-boggling variety” of accents, vocabulary and grammar. In the middle of it all, the standard to “find and stick to” is the one “which can take us to everyone”, even if we sound slightly different to each other (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). The aim is to be understood by everyone, not to copy one country’s accent.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slides link two talks by people of global reach. <strong>Mother Teresa’s Nobel Prize acceptance speech</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=237s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:57</a>): born to an Albanian family in Skopje (now in North Macedonia; the lecture says Albania), she worked in India for over 50 years and gave her prize money to the poor. <strong>Indra Nooyi in conversation with the Asia Society</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=318s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:18</a>): educated in India, she rose to head PepsiCo while caring for a large family. It is not impossible, she says, “so long as you know what you want to achieve and you are willing to work for that”. Listening to such people improves “your values and priorities” and “also your English” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">In learning to speak, remember the following</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides’ points, in order (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=444s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:24</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Have something to say.</li>\n<li>Politeness is more important than even pronunciation and grammar.</li>\n<li>Slow tempo is a marker of politeness. (<em>Tempo</em> = the speed at which you speak.)</li>\n<li>In English, different words are stressed differently.</li>\n<li>Vowel sounds in English are very long or very short.</li>\n<li>Good spoken English is a product of good listening habits.</li>\n<li>Books and a dictionary to learn from.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"have-something-to-say\">Have something to say</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>After the greetings (<em>good morning, namaste, vanakkam, sat sri akal, assalamualaikum</em>), what next? “Above and before anything else we must have <strong>something to say</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>): something <strong>interesting</strong>, <strong>relevant</strong> and <strong>helpful</strong> to your listeners, said in an interesting manner. “If you have these things, all else can follow: accent, grammar, vocabulary” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Jesus Christ, Prophet Muhammad and Gautama Buddha are known all over the world, yet many of us do not know which language they spoke. They are known because “they had something to say” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). A good accent “will be a great help, but a greater help will be to cultivate the habit of learning”, of gaining knowledge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). Lecture 4.4 returns to this.</p>\n<h3 id=\"politeness\">Politeness</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Politeness comes first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Listeners “will pardon you for mistakes of grammar, for mistakes of vocabulary … very easily with a smile. You just say sorry and you get across. But they won’t pardon you for being <strong>rude</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). (<em>Rude</em> = impolite, without respect.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Many Indian languages show respect inside the verb: one form for close friends, another for respect. (For example, Hindi <em>baitho</em> and <em>baithiye</em> both mean “sit”; the example is mine.) English has only one verb form. It once had two pronouns, <em>thou</em> (familiar) and <em>you</em> (respectful), but it “moved with democracy” to <strong>one pronoun for all</strong>: the king and the king’s driver are both <em>you</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). That does not make English rude. It shows politeness in other ways (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Polite words</td>\n<td><em>please, thanks, excuse me, sorry, pardon me</em> before or after <strong>every</strong> request, even to friends and family</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intonation</td>\n<td>say it gently: <em>Will you please sit down?</em>, not a blunt <em>Come, sit down</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question form</td>\n<td>give every request or command as a <strong>question</strong>; only army commanders give bare orders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow tempo</td>\n<td>speak slowly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Commands sound rude in English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Not <em>Tell me time, please</em> but <em>Will you tell me the time, please?</em> Not <em>Stop now</em> but <em>Will you please stop now?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). Listen for such phrases in English films and documentaries: “these things are so easy to learn and so easy to use” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"slow-tempo\">Slow tempo</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Slow tempo is a marker of politeness</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=828s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:48</a>): “Slow tempo helps both, speakers and listeners. English is a slow tempo language. English speakers pause after phrase, clause &amp; sentence.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>People find rapid speakers hard to follow, and they think them impolite. Slow speech may feel artificial at first, and people may laugh, “but soon they will imitate you”: it is “your best guarantee to be understood worldwide” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Many Asian languages pause only at the <strong>end</strong> of a sentence; English also pauses <strong>inside</strong> it: <em>There was a king # and he had three daughters ##</em> (# marks a pause; Lecture 4.3 explains the marks) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). In global English this “is obligatory”: a brief but <strong>audible</strong> pause, “maybe a couple of seconds or even less”, after each phrase, clause and sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"word-stress-varies\">Word stress varies</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Variant word stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “In English, different words are stressed differently” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1017s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:57</a>). A <strong>syllable</strong> is one beat of a word (<em>Lon-don</em> has two). The <strong>stressed syllable</strong> is the beat said with more force. In English it may be the first, a middle or the last syllable, so you learn it word by word. The lecture calls this “a peculiar problem” of English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples, with capitals for the stressed syllable (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First syllable</th>\n<th>Second syllable</th>\n<th>Last syllable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>LON-don (not lon-DON)</td>\n<td>ber-LIN</td>\n<td>ja-PAN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IN-di-a</td>\n<td>ne-PAL</td>\n<td>bra-ZIL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U-nion</td>\n<td>u-NI-ted</td>\n<td>pa-ki-STAN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Union</em> and <em>united</em> both begin with <em>uni-</em>, yet the stress falls in different places. Stress returns in <a href=\"#/english1/w08\">Week 8</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">No grammatical gender</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Hindi you must learn that tea and sugar are feminine and salt is masculine; in French even the article changes (<em>le garçon</em>, <em>la fille</em>). English has no grammatical gender: <em>the boy, the girl, the boss, the aunt</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). Its difficulty lies in word stress instead.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"long-and-short-vowels\">Long and short vowels</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vowel length</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “Vowel sounds in English are very long or very short” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1017s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:57</a>). Some are “almost twice as long” as the vowels of many Indian languages; others are hardly heard (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Length</th>\n<th>The lecture’s words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Very long: open the jaw wide and hold it</td>\n<td><em>car</em> /kɑː/, <em>market</em> /ˈmɑːkɪt/: <em>I come to office daily in a car</em> (standard English: <em>to the office</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>So long that it becomes two vowels</td>\n<td><em>boat</em> /bəʊt/: <em>How do you cross the river? In a boat.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very short, hardly heard</td>\n<td>the <em>a-</em> of <em>about, away, arise</em> /ə/; the <em>be-</em> of <em>before, behind, below, beneath, between</em> /bɪ/. But <em>-fore, -low, -tween</em> are long (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>We need not make the long vowels as long as an English speaker does, “but definitely longer than it is generally in our accents”. “These things are learned with drill in no time.” (The IPA symbols between slashes are explained in <a href=\"#/english1/w01\">Week 1</a>.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"good-listening-habits\">Good listening habits</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Good spoken English is a product of good listening habits</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1302s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:42</a>). Teachers are only coaches: “just as I cannot walk for you … she cannot speak for you. You have to do that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). Listen widely (songs, films, documentaries, match commentary), and part of the time pay attention to the <strong>manner</strong> of speech (how they pronounce, which word they use), not just the <strong>matter</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). We know no language that we did not first hear or read: listening comes before speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Manhattan exercise (the lecture’s task at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide links the song “Manhattan” from the film <em>English Vinglish</em>, about a housewife who goes to New York speaking no English and becomes fluent within weeks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1398s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:18</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Enjoy the song once.</li>\n<li>Listen again for only two words, <strong>New York</strong> and <strong>Manhattan</strong>, which you hear in many accents.</li>\n<li>Pause, and note how many different tones and accents you heard.</li>\n<li>Play it again to check.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> once your ears catch many tunes, your mind “will immediately catch the tune you should use in a particular situation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"books-a-dictionary-and-a-daily-drill\">Books, a dictionary and a daily drill</h3>\n<p>English has more publications than any other language; India alone produces more than 50,000 English books a year (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>). The books slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1557s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:57</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>Better Spoken English</strong></em> by Shreesh Chaudhary (Vikas Publishing). It lists about <strong>600 to 700 long words</strong> (more than one syllable, like <em>monkey, elephant, university</em>) that IIT Madras students often used, and often mispronounced, in their presentations (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>An <em>Advanced Learner’s Dictionary of English</em>, any edition</strong> (Oxford, Collins, Cambridge, Longman). It gives pronunciation, usage and meaning, everything except <strong>etymology</strong> (where a word comes from). Keep one on your phone and check whenever you are in doubt (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Your own list, 20 minutes a day</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>List the words you use often, check them in the dictionary, and drill the ones you say wrongly: <strong>10 minutes in the morning and 10 minutes in the evening</strong>. Within a few weeks you get them right, and other words with the same pattern too: fix the vowel of <em>cat</em>, and <em>rat, sat, mat, hat</em> follow (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"speak-slowly\">Speak slowly</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.2. “The best way any speaker can be understood by any listener is by <strong>speaking slowly</strong>”, much more slowly than many of us in Asian and African countries do (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The lecture says why, and how to build the habit.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">English is a slow-tempo language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=84s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:24</a>): “English is a slow tempo language. English speakers pause after groups of words, after each clause, after each sentence.” There are two reasons. English has many <strong>long vowels</strong>. And it pauses <strong>inside</strong> the sentence, at the end of a <strong>phrase</strong> (a group of words that works as one unit) and of a <strong>clause</strong> (a group of words with its own verb); Asian languages mostly pause only at the end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two speakers at a good tempo (the slide’s audio samples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Martin Luther King Jr.</strong>, with the longest silences of the recording marked: <em>I have a dream # that one day on the red hills of Georgia # the sons of former slaves and the sons of former slave owners # will be able to sit down together at the table of brotherhood ##</em> This “is good tempo”: almost anyone would understand us at this speed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Colin Firth</strong> accepting the Best Actor Oscar: about 40 seconds of thanks, “the kind of tempo we should use when we speak on public occasions, in formal situations, when we make presentations” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “Slow speech is our best chance to be understood.” In the time King takes, some of us “would produce maybe 100 words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Counting King’s words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture says King produces “only about 20 words” in about 20 seconds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). By my count the sentence has 37 words (47 syllables) and takes about 17 seconds: under 3 syllables a second. What makes it so easy to follow is the long pause between one group of words and the next.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pause is the punctuation of spoken language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=402s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:42</a>): “Pause is like the punctuation mark of spoken language.” Words flow one after another; without pauses “it is not very clear to the listener which word belongs to which group” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One sentence, two meanings (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=402s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:42</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“‘A woman without her man is nothing.’ This sentence can have different meanings depending upon how you pause.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Spoken with pauses at the commas</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A woman, without her man, is nothing.</em></td>\n<td>a woman is nothing without her man</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A woman, without her, man is nothing.</em></td>\n<td>a man is nothing without a woman</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>In the first, <em>without her man</em> is one group describing <em>a woman</em>, so <em>is nothing</em> is said of the woman.</li>\n<li>In the second, <em>without her</em> is one group and <em>man is nothing</em> is a new group, so <em>nothing</em> is said of <em>man</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the same words give opposite meanings, and only the pauses tell them apart. The slide adds: “Find other examples.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rapid speech distorts sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=522s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:42</a>): “Rapid speech causes distortions in sounds; they merge into one another, some sounds are dropped, some sounds change form and shape, and become difficult to understand.” It is like a railway compartment meant for 20 people carrying 200 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The para jump strips (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Rapid speech, written as letters: <strong>Icanneverforgetmyfirstparajump</strong>. Can you read it? Perhaps, with effort, in a minute.</li>\n<li>The words separated: <strong>I can never forget my first para jump.</strong> Clear “in one look” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=645s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:45</a>).</li>\n<li>Slow speech, with pauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=666s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:06</a>): <strong>I can never forget # my first para jump # There were seven of us # each more scared # than the others #</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> pauses do for the ear what spaces do for the eye. (A <em>para jump</em> is a parachute jump.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>We know how speech is made (the lungs push out air; the vocal cords, tongue and jaws shape it) and how it travels through the air. “But we know nearly nothing about how listeners understand these sounds.” We know that the sound reaches the <strong>cochlea</strong> (the hearing organ inside the ear), but not what in the mind then tells <em>road</em> from <em>load</em>, or <em>wet</em> from <em>vet</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). So give the listener time.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">First things first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Speaking slowly “is much more important than … learning the pronunciation of any one particular sound or word” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). The lecture ends on the same point (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"slow-speech-is-polite-speech\">Slow speech is polite speech</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Slow speech is easy, polite, and helps the speaker</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=711s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:51</a>): “Slow speech is easy for the listener to understand. It is a mark of politeness. Slow speech gives the speaker time to think and use correct words in a correct manner.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Language goes with culture: we say <em>namaste</em> with folded hands and a bow. In English-speaking cultures <strong>speaking fast is not polite</strong>. It seems that you are in a hurry and are not giving the listener enough time, “and therefore enough respect” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The slide links a <strong>fast</strong> and a <strong>slow</strong> sample (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). The fast one is a man racing through a story (more than a hundred words in about 15 seconds, by my count). The slow one is a calm news item, played twice: <em>Kamikatsu in Japan is fast moving towards becoming the country’s first zero-waste municipality …</em> Which could you follow?</p>\n<p>Speak slowly and respectfully, and you can talk to a prince or the Pope; even your mistakes of grammar may be pardoned (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). Slow speech helps you too: you “get time to think … to hear your own word before you speak” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-learn-to-speak-slowly\">How to learn to speak slowly</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Changing a lifelong habit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1164s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:24</a>): “It may not be easy to learn to speak slowly; changing habit of a life time can be difficult. But it is not impossible.” And: “If we learn one thing about Spoken English, we must learn to speak slowly, comfortably. All else will follow.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your tempo is the same in every language.</strong> If you speak fast in English, you speak fast in your mother tongue too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). So practise slow speech in your mother tongue as well, with friends and family. They may laugh, and then imitate you (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Switch.</strong> Be your natural self in informal talk. Use the slow tempo with strangers, in formal situations and in presentations, “just like switching between two languages or two dialects” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). Determined practice takes <strong>2 to 3 weeks</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The self-test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s test for a rapid speaker (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take an easy printed paragraph in English and <strong>count its words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Record yourself on your phone, <strong>reading it the way you speak</strong>.</li>\n<li>Note the <strong>time</strong> taken, and compare the words with the time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you are a rapid speaker, remind yourself after every group of words: “I am going to take time; I am going to speak slowly” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">How fast is too fast?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s limits (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>What the lecture says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>over 3 or 4 <strong>syllables a second</strong></td>\n<td>you are a rapid speaker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>over <strong>100 words a minute</strong></td>\n<td>you are “definitely” a rapid speaker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>over <strong>300 words a minute</strong></td>\n<td>research: not understood even by listeners from the speaker’s own community, “but even that is rapid”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>under <strong>100 words a minute</strong></td>\n<td>the rule of the <strong>International Civil Aviation Organization</strong> for pilots talking to air traffic controllers; pilots are tested, and those who speak rapidly fail (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Words per minute = number of words ÷ seconds taken × 60. A worked case is in Check yourself below.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise: the lecture’s activity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1404s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:24</a>): record a one-minute speech on your favourite topic and count the words. Record it again on the same topic, slowly, and count again (“you may have fewer words”). Ask a listener which is understood better. And listen to “good” speakers on television: are they slow or fast? Newsreaders, presenters and great public speakers “all speak slowly” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"phrasal-pause\">Phrasal pause</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.3. A <strong>pause</strong> is “a temporary stop” in speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Standard English pauses not only at the end of a sentence but also inside it, after a <strong>clause</strong> and after a <strong>phrase</strong>; the lecture calls this unique to English among the languages it knows. Hindi says <em>ek raja tha</em> in one go; English says <em>Once upon a time # there was a king # The king had three daughters</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=51s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:51</a>): “Two or more words behaving in a sentence like one word make phrase.” <em>An apple, a big basket, a committee of experts</em> “all behave in a sentence just like a single noun”: <em>Fish swims</em>, <em>An apple sells</em>, <em>A big basket has been kept at the temple</em>, <em>A committee of experts has been formed</em>. One word can be a phrase too: a phrase is “one, two or more words which together behave like one word, one unit” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>In <em>A big basket has been kept at the temple</em>, <em>a big basket</em> is a <strong>noun phrase</strong> (it does the job of a noun), and <em>at the temple</em> is a <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> (it begins with the preposition <em>at</em> and tells where) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrasal pause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A pause after a phrase is a <strong>phrasal pause</strong>: “a unique feature” of the standard varieties of spoken English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=297s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:57</a>): “Speakers of standard English pause also after a phrase, just as they pause after a clause and a sentence.” The slides mark a short pause with <strong>#</strong> and a longer pause, at the end of a sentence, with <strong>##</strong>. (Some books use / and // instead.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The All India Radio news (the lecture’s recording at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture plays a news bulletin twice, the second time stopping at each pause. The slide’s marking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>):</p>\n<p><em>Good evening # this is All India Radio # and I am Anuja Kumar # with the news at nine ## Prime Minister Narendra Modi today stated # that India is one of the safest habitats in the world # with almost three thousand tigers …</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Good evening #</em>: pause after a <strong>greeting</strong>. “Don’t begin at once; give a second” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>this is All India Radio #</em>: pause after an <strong>identity</strong>, as in <em>I am Shreesh Chaudhary # I am an English teacher</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Prime Minister Narendra Modi today stated #</em>: a <strong>clause</strong>. The next clause says what was stated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “every unit is followed by a pause”, well before the sentence ends.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three lengths and one rule</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Each pause is a little longer than the one before, and none is long (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>):</p>\n<p><strong>phrase pause &lt; clause pause &lt; sentence pause</strong></p>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=669s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:09</a>): “This pause is not long, but it is noticeable. It is the punctuation of speech. … Pause in this manner follows a simple rule: pause after every phrase, or every group of words within a phrase, if the phrase is a big group of words.” So <em>A committee of experts #</em> takes one pause, and <em>A committee of experts # that specialises in botany #</em> takes two (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). These are conventions, “no hard and fast rules”, but with pauses “your chances of being understood will be greater” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"where-to-pause\">Where to pause</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pause after</th>\n<th>A slide example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a word of address</td>\n<td><em>May I borrow this book # professor #</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>every 2 or 3 digits</td>\n<td><em>94 # 43 # 02 # 84 # 34</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>each level of an address</td>\n<td><em>D4/12 # 2nd Link Road # IIT Campus #</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>so, but, therefore</em> and the like</td>\n<td><em>so # invest in your health ##</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>each item in a series</td>\n<td><em>corns # milk # eggs # bread #</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>every phrase and clause</td>\n<td><em>The black rose # is the loveliest of flowers ##</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Words of address.</strong> A <strong>word of address</strong> is the name or title you use when you speak <em>to</em> someone: <em>Liz, John, professor, doctor, sir, Governor, my dear</em>. “Pause after a word of address is essential” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=729s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:09</a>): <em>May I borrow this book # professor #</em> and <em>Do you # doctor # know why I got fever? ##</em> In the middle of a sentence, pause before and after it. The lecture’s spoken examples, marked by the same rule: <em>Will you # Liz # wait for me?</em> and <em>So kind of you # Governor</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Numbers.</strong> “Pause after a pair of numbers, or three numbers, is helpful” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=822s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:42</a>). Say each digit. The phone number 9443028434 becomes <em>94 # 43 # 02 # 84 # 34</em>, or <em>944 # 302 # 84 # 34</em>. Likewise <em>The postal code of the area I live in is # 600 # 113 ##</em> and <em>My employee id is # 19 # 95 ##</em>. Then “you really don’t have to repeat yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Addresses.</strong> “Pause after every new level, e.g. door no., street, area, city, state”, going from the lowest unit up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=906s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:06</a>): <em>My address is # D4/12 # 2nd Link Road # IIT Campus # Chennai 600 # 036</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Discourse markers.</strong></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Discourse marker</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A word or phrase that signals a turn in the talk: a conclusion (<em>so, therefore, thus</em>), a contrast (<em>but</em>), or emphasis (<em>of course, indeed, by all means</em>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>): after such words “also you should take a pause”, even after one word, because it “signals change in discourse” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Without good health # there is no happiness ## so # invest in your health ##</em></li>\n<li><em>Ruth was tired # but # not bored ##</em> (<em>but</em> brings a contrast)</li>\n<li><em>Many students rise late # therefore # they come to class without breakfast ##</em> (<em>therefore</em> draws a conclusion) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Words in a series.</strong> “After every word in a series, you should pause” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1098s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:18</a>). Run together, the list may still be understood, “but it may not be good”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>For breakfast # I take some corns # milk # eggs # bread # butter # fruits # and tea ##</em></li>\n<li><em>I am doing honours in Economics # but # I am also interested in History # Philosophy # Malayalam Literature # and Raja Ravi Varma’s paintings ##</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Every phrase and clause.</strong> “Ordinarily, the rule is: after every Noun Phrase, Verb Phrase, Adjectival Phrase and Adverbial Phrase” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1206s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:06</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Roses (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1206s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:06</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Rose # is a lovely flower ##</em></li>\n<li><em>The black rose # is the loveliest of flowers ##</em></li>\n<li><em>All the beautiful # black roses # that came from Bangalore yesterday # are # many say # extra-ordinarily lovely flowers ##</em></li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Each sentence begins with its subject, a <strong>noun phrase</strong>: <em>Rose</em>, <em>The black rose</em>, <em>All the beautiful black roses that came from Bangalore yesterday</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The third noun phrase is long, so it takes pauses inside it, after each smaller unit.</li>\n<li><em>many say</em> (= according to many people) is <strong>parenthetic</strong>: words dropped into the sentence as if in brackets. Pauses set it off (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a pause after each unit, and extra pauses inside a long unit.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pauses are the commas you speak</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In writing we put commas; in speech we do not say “comma”, we pause. “Pause is the punctuation mark of speech” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>): phrases and clauses are all followed by a pause, and “this shows which words go together as a group”:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>The units</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>He # that is down # needs fear no fall ##</em></td>\n<td><em>He</em> + <em>that is down</em> (describes <em>he</em>) + the rest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Strike the iron # while it is hot ##</em></td>\n<td>a clause + <em>while it is hot</em> (says <strong>when</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Only the wearer knows # where shoe pinches ##</em></td>\n<td>a clause + a clause (says <strong>what</strong> the wearer knows)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Rabindranath Tagore # the Nobel laureate # was also a freedom fighter ##</em></td>\n<td>the name + a phrase about him + the rest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>All # that glitters # is not gold ##</em></td>\n<td>the main clause <em>All … is not gold</em>; <em>that glitters</em> describes <em>All</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In my words, the sayings mean: a humble person need not fear a fall; act at the right moment; only the sufferer knows the pain; what shines is not always valuable.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The slides’ wording</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The pauses are the point, but a few slide sentences are not standard English. Say <em>A fish swims</em> (or <em>Fish swim</em>), <em>The rose is a lovely flower</em>, <em>some corn</em> or <em>cornflakes</em> (<em>corns</em> are patches of hard skin on the feet), <em>why I got a fever</em>, and <em>where the shoe pinches</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s practice set (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“For practice, mark and speak the following sentences.” The lecture’s answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1581s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:21</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>May I come in # sir?</em></li>\n<li><em>Stars twinkle # planets do not.</em></li>\n<li><em>Fault # dear Brutus # is not in our stars.</em> (after Shakespeare’s <em>Julius Caesar</em>, which has <em>The fault</em>)</li>\n<li><em>Long years ago # we made a tryst with destiny.</em> (from Nehru’s speech at Independence; a <em>tryst</em> is a promise to meet)</li>\n<li><em>I have a dream # that one day # sons of slaves # and sons of slave owners # would sit together # on the table of fraternity.</em> (<em>fraternity</em> = brotherhood; King’s own words are <em>at the table of brotherhood</em>: we sit <em>at</em> a table, not <em>on</em> it)</li>\n<li><em>Given a chance # everyone would like to go to school.</em></li>\n<li><em>More girls than boys # drop out of school # before completing secondary education.</em></li>\n<li><em>Ask not # what the nation can do for you # ask # what you can do for the nation.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> each pause falls at the end of a unit (an address, a phrase or a clause), never inside one. Practise until it is a habit, and you will be “a lot better understood”, even by a crowd (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"do-you-have-something-to-say\">Do you have something to say?</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.4 is about <strong>content</strong>. “We speak to tell something”, so the big question in formal speaking is: <strong>do you have something to say?</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why we speak</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=18s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:18</a>): “We speak to give or get information, answer, news, knowledge, joy, to hurt, heal, entertain, encourage, discourage, persuade, convince, dissuade…” The purposes are more than we can count (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>), so be clear about yours. Some of the lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>to <strong>discourage</strong>: <em>Don’t smoke; it can cause cancer.</em></li>\n<li>to <strong>convince</strong>: <em>Buy this brand of soap; it is very skin friendly and it cleans your clothes like nothing else.</em></li>\n<li>to <strong>dissuade</strong> (advise someone against something): <em>Why do you need to buy another shirt? You already have too many.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Content makes great speakers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=174s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:54</a>): “All the great speakers whom we know because of what they had to say.” “Contents are important, more important than pronunciation, than grammar, than vocabulary” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M. K. Gandhi</strong>: “An eye for an eye only ends up making the whole world blind.” The lecturer does not know in which of his languages Gandhi said it, yet he has known the line since childhood (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Nelson Mandela</strong>: “Do not judge me by my successes, judge me by how many times I fell down and got back up again” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=285s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:45</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Alexander Pope</strong>, on the scientist Isaac Newton: “Nature and Nature’s laws lay hid in Night: God said, Let Newton be! and all was light” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=378s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:18</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"prepare\">Prepare</h3>\n<p>If you must speak <strong>impromptu</strong> (on the spot, with no preparation; also called <strong>extempore</strong>), “trust your wit and go ahead”. If you have time, then however much you know, “check again, prepare again” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). A short time is no excuse: the lecture notes that Nobel laureates get under a minute to accept the prize, “and some of them have said great things in those minutes” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A two-minute talk on a civic problem</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=444s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:24</a>): for a talk on “A Civic Problem in Your Area” (<em>civic</em> = to do with a town and its people), you can say:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Where?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>What?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>How much?</strong> <strong>Quantification</strong>: how big the problem is, in numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Its effects</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two minutes is short, so “you must mention at least the following” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=753s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:33</a>): the <strong>name of the problem</strong>, the <strong>name of the place</strong>, the <strong>extent of the problem, quantity</strong>, and <strong>its harmful impact</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Flooding, short and long (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The problem and the place.</strong> Short: <em>Flood is an annual problem in Bihar.</em> Long: <em>Flooding is a huge problem in my village in Bihar, near the Indo-Nepal border. Floods come every year in the month of August and cause untold misery to people there.</em></li>\n<li><strong>What happens</strong>, only if there is time: rivers overflow their embankments and enter homes, farms, roads and railways.</li>\n<li><strong>The effects</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). Short: <em>Thousands of people die in the wake of floods</em> (<em>in the wake of</em> = soon after). Long: floods ruin agriculture, affect traffic and cause diseases; cattle and people die for want of food and water; epidemics such as typhoid and cholera follow.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “It’s like budgeting.” Ask yourself “How much can I say in the given time?”, and fit your talk to it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Never exceed your time</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Time is in finite supply”: no matter how strong you are, you cannot stretch a day into 25 hours. Respect the time you are given (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The waterlogged road (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=822s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:42</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point</th>\n<th>What to say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem</td>\n<td>Water logged road</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Place</td>\n<td>On 3rd Cross Road, Nehru Nagar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extent</td>\n<td>Knee-deep water even after an hour long rain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Support it with a photograph in a slide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impact</td>\n<td>Spreads disease, smell, and stops free flow of traffic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture adds that vehicles collide and burn more petrol, pedestrians and cattle cannot move freely, and people catch infections (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Check your words too: the lecturer first wrote “water logging on the road”, and the dictionary showed that <strong>waterlogged road</strong> is better. “If you have the attitude to check, you learn all the time” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"quantify-and-find-the-data\">Quantify, and find the data</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quantification</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“There is no problem faced by human beings that cannot be quantified, and your presentation becomes better when you talk through quantification” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). For the waterlogged road: how many accidents, how much longer vehicles took to cross, how many people got skin infections or fever, how many slipped and fell (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). Do not just say <em>It’s noisy</em>; say <em>Even at five in the morning the locality has 54 decibels of noise coming from the temple next to my house</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). (A <em>decibel</em> is the unit of loudness.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where to find what to say</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>): “If you do not have enough to say, you may find out.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Search engines on the Net: Google, Wikipedia, other sites.</li>\n<li>Local sources: libraries and archives. In Chennai, for example, the Connemara Library, the Anna Centenary Library and the university libraries; many are now digitised (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Local folklore and songs.</li>\n<li>Elders, knowledgeable local senior people. (The lecturer has heard that Chennai had no water problem until about 1960, because its rivers, the Cooum and the Adyar, carried fresh water. He has not seen that himself, so he can talk to older residents and read older books, where he finds, for example, boats bringing rice, vegetables, fruits and banana leaves from Andhra to Chennai (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“I already know enough”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Check anyway: a new photograph, or a second look at the same source, shows something new (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Know as much as you can, then choose how much of it fits the time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-interesting-and-relevant\">Make it interesting and relevant</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What makes a talk interesting</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1296s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:36</a>): “Whatever you say must be ‘interesting’ to the listener.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Good news</strong>: a job, an admission, a prize, your team winning the cup (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A good word</strong>: <em>Well done! Congratulations!</em> Praise “creates in the listener a favourable, a positive mindset”, so whatever you say next is heard (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Entertainment</strong>: a joke, a song, a story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a civic problem, show <strong>how it hurts the listeners</strong> and what it costs them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Praise, yes; false flattery, no</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Never say things that are false.” You do not need to: everyone has something good in them to praise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). Avoid harmful, violent or slanderous gossip, and do not make yourself ridiculous. A joke is “like salt in your food”: too much and the dish is ruined (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Show it: photographs, data, maps</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“You can also back it up with a photograph” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1557s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:57</a>). A photo of a flooded road conveys in a moment what words take minutes to describe (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Give data in a way people feel. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1476s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:36</a>): say how many vehicles the slippery, submerged road has caused “to collide, to turn upside down”, and how many people, “including women, old men &amp; women and children”, it has made “slip and fall down”.</li>\n<li>A Google map shows where your area is, even to people who have never been there (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Be relevant</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1647s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:27</a>): “Whatever you say must be ‘relevant’ to the listener. There is no point talking about ‘flying to fish’, or ‘swimming to bird’, etc. It helps to research or re-check your facts.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The right word: dictionary and thesaurus</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1707s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:27</a>): “See Dictionary, Roget’s Thesaurus for the appropriate word for ‘water-logging’.” A <strong>thesaurus</strong> lists words of similar meaning and helps you choose between them, because similar is not the same: <em>fine, nice, good, excellent, marvellous</em>; <em>aroma</em> or <em>fragrance</em>; <em>stink</em> or <em>stench</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The last slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1782s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:42</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1809s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:09</a>): check what harm such spots can do; check how engineers measure the water, and decide how you will convey it; and “if you have limited time for presentation, you should check your facts and how much to say much more carefully”. Present the data from your own angle: an engineer like an engineer, a doctor like a doctor, a social worker like a social worker (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rehearse, then cut your coat</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Then you should preferably by rehearsing decide what and how much of what you are going to say” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1818s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:18</a>): “cut your coat according to your cloth” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). The lecture’s activity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=1839s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:39</a>): gather information about a man-made civic problem in your area. Talk about it to a friend with a timer, never exceeding the time “even by a second”. Record yourself, collect more information, talk again, and compare the two recordings. Then repeat with another listener.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"effective-presentations\">Effective presentations</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.5. In many professions (sales, teaching, administration, research) we present often, and our success depends partly on it: did the listeners understand us, were they convinced? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Effective presentation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): “You make an effective presentation when listeners listen to you with attention. Then you can persuade, or entertain, or inspire, or convince, etc. Listeners may or may not always agree with you, but they see your position, your point of view, and they can listen to you.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"stage-manners\">Stage manners</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stage manners</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=123s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:03</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=177s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:57</a>): “All conversations are presentations. All presentations are stage performances. All presenters are performers.” And: “Whether talking to a friend or a crowd, you perform an act. So like all ‘actors’, you have to follow some stage manners. We must be <strong>well-turned out: washed, groomed and dressed</strong>.” (<em>Well turned out</em> = neat and suitably dressed.) Like actors, we must mind the words, the costume, the occasion, the place, the stage, the voice and the props (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Dress for the role</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=285s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:45</a>): “You need not be over-dressed, you must not be under-dressed, you should be appropriately dressed.” Its pictures put fashionable casual clothes under “Inappropriate”: fine for the street, but not for presenting “a business project of a couple of million rupees”. Under “Appropriate” are a sari and business suits. Every community has its own formal dress; follow its standard, as actors dress for their role (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"relax-and-smile\">Relax and smile</h3>\n<p>Nerves before a presentation are natural; even teachers with 40 years of experience feel them. <strong>Smile, take a deep breath, and greet your audience with a smile</strong>: “with that smile your tension, your stiffness disappears” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">People see more than they listen</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=384s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:24</a>): “Relax, make yourself comfortable. If you are tense, nervous, sleepy, drowsy, distracted, you may not make an effective presentation. People see more than they listen.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The finger experiment (the lecture’s demonstration at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The experiment comes from the website of Toastmasters International, a club for public speaking.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The speaker makes a circle with his fingers and moves it slowly anticlockwise; you copy him.</li>\n<li>He <strong>says</strong> “slowly bring it to your <strong>chin</strong>”, but he puts it on his <strong>cheek</strong>.</li>\n<li>In his classes, out of 50 students only two or three touched the chin; over 45 touched the cheek (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> people follow what they <strong>see</strong> more than what they <strong>hear</strong>. “Nothing explains the power of eyes more than this experiment.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"posture-hands-and-eyes\">Posture, hands and eyes</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Relaxed but alert</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Posture</strong> is the way you hold your body. Do not be sleepy, and do not be stiff “as if you are going to be executed any moment”. As Gautama Buddha taught, the truth is at neither extreme: be <strong>relaxed but alert</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). The slides show sitting and standing postures (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=591s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:51</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=633s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:33</a>). You need not stand like a soldier, but do not slump or stand on one leg; sit upright with your shoulders straight (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Greet, and use your hands with meaning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=708s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:48</a>): “Greet your audience with a friendly manner. Use your hands in a meaningful manner.” Begin with a smile: <em>Good morning. Today we are going to look at a problem that has bothered us all for a long time …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Hands locked in front or behind, in your pockets, or on the podium: “none of these postures by itself is good or bad” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). <strong>Mindless flailing</strong>, waving your hands at every word, wastes energy and distracts the audience. Use them with meaning: one hand for each side of a choice, as in <em>On the one hand is the question of cost; on the other hand is the question of time</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Eye contact</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides: “Eye-contact keeps the listener with the speaker” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>), and “You lose eye contact, and you lose your audience” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1104s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:24</a>). With a large audience, <strong>pan slowly</strong>: talk to one part for 30 to 40 seconds, then turn slowly to the next. Do not stare at one person until they are embarrassed, and do not dart your eyes from side to side (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"voice\">Voice</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Voice is a tool</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1119s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:39</a>): “Voice is an important tool for presentations. You can have loud and soft voice, rapid and slow voice, confident and doubtful or confused voice, giving a variety of signals to the listener. You must learn to <strong>modulate</strong> your voice.” (<em>Modulate</em> = change the loudness, speed and tone of your voice to suit what you say.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>With your voice you can bring tears, anger, inspiration or laughter. Gandhiji, a small, thin man, moved a whole subcontinent: “there is something in his voice” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). Learn from actors: everything else is given to them, “but voice is their very own” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). Slow speech with <strong>appropriate pauses, tone and tempo</strong> earns respect even when you make an occasional mistake (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Malala Yousafzai (the slide’s clip of her Nobel Prize speech at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1272s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:12</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>She is very young, but confident and well trained (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Not every word has the standard pronunciation (the lecture points to <em>fact</em>), and there is an occasional slip of grammar, “but that hardly matters: look at what she has to say”. The world can no longer deny half of humanity its chance.</li>\n<li>She has humour: a Nobel Peace Prize winner “who still fights with her brothers” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> content, confidence, a relaxed manner and humour make the effect, not perfect pronunciation. Entertain your listeners, but “not in a vulgar manner, not in a superficial, not in a shallow manner” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"slides\">Slides</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not overload your slides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>): “Do not overload your PPT slides. They lack adequate display time.” “Ordinarily, it is good to mention only a few bullet points in one slide, and only a few words in each line.” “Read each slide silently and give as much time for each slide as at least you take to read one.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Unstructured and structured slides (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1524s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:24</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1596s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:36</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Unstructured</strong>: “How Rivers Are Formed”, six long bullets of small, pale text on a green photograph. “You need half an hour to read this”, and the pale colours on green make it worse (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>). It is a page of a book pasted on a photograph, “so that you lose both the photograph and the text” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Structured</strong>: “Year Over Year Growth”, a bar chart of the industry (white bars) against Mac (brown bars):</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quarter</th>\n<th>Industry</th>\n<th>Mac</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Q3 2006</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q4 2006</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>28%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q1 2007</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n<td>36%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q2 2007</td>\n<td>13%</td>\n<td>33%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>One remark is enough: “Macintosh grew faster than the rest of the industry” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> slides are the best tool for numbers and charts, not for pages of text. Do not read every word aloud (unless a visually challenged guest is present); point to one or two highlighted words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Colour combinations</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1773s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:33</a>): “Colour combinations are not unwelcome in PPt slides”, if they serve communication. Yellow-green on dark green “doesn’t mean anything”; black on white you see best. Indian Railways station boards, <strong>jet black on turmeric yellow</strong>, can be read from far away. Turmeric yellow on mustard yellow? “Only Picasso can read it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Creativity, language and data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1857s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:57</a>): “Effective presentation requires an intelligent combination of creativity, language and data.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creativity</strong>: using old things, can you say something new?</li>\n<li><strong>Language</strong>: a good command of it, but <strong>simple, easy, conversational</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data</strong>: what you say “should not be mere prejudice” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Speaking is a skill</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Like cycling or swimming, nobody can teach it to you. You fall, you get up, you trust yourself. <strong>Prepare, trust yourself, practise</strong>, and speak whenever you get the chance, even if you make mistakes at first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0jkrPkUbTg&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"telephone-english\">Telephone English</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.6. You either <strong>make a call</strong> (you dial the number) or <strong>take a call</strong> (you <strong>pick up</strong> the ringing phone) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Calls to a boss, colleagues or a company are <strong>formal</strong>; calls to family and friends are <strong>informal</strong>. Each step of a call has set phrases.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"making-a-call\">Making a call</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Starting a call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Greet, then introduce yourself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=15s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:15</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Formal</th>\n<th>Informal (with replies)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hi. / Hello.</em></td>\n<td><em>How is it going?</em> <em>Good, thanks. What about you?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>This is Megha from Airvoice Services.</em></td>\n<td><em>How are you?</em> <em>Great, thanks. What about you?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>My name is Megha. I am calling from Airvoice Services.</em></td>\n<td><em>What’s up?</em> <em>Not much. The usual.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Informal greetings are for friends</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>What’s up?</em> and <em>How is it going?</em> belong to calls with friends. With a friend “you wouldn’t say: my name is this and I’m calling for XYZ purpose” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>), and on a formal call you give your name and company.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Asking for someone</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=147s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:27</a>). You call your friend Padmini:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Formal (a colleague answers her office phone)</th>\n<th>Informal (her sister, your friend, answers at home)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>May I speak to Padmini?</em></td>\n<td><em>Is Padmini there?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Could I speak with Padmini?</em></td>\n<td><em>Is Padmini around?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I’d like to speak to Padmini, please.</em></td>\n<td><em>Can I talk to Padmini?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>On a formal call you may add <em>please</em> at the end: <em>May I speak to Padmini, please?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The purpose of the call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=246s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:06</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I’m calling to ask about…</em>, as in <em>I’m calling to ask about your software consulting services.</em></li>\n<li><em>Could you tell me…?</em>, as in <em>Could you tell me how much it costs?</em></li>\n<li>Informal: <em>I just wanted to ask…</em>, as in <em>I just wanted to ask if you’re free to meet sometime this week.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Availability and messages</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When the person is out or busy, the slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=312s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:12</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=393s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:33</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>When would be a good time to call?</em> / <em>When will he / she be back?</em> / <em>Please let me know your best convenient time.</em> (Standard English says <em>a convenient time</em> or <em>the best time</em>; <em>best convenient</em> is Indian English.) Informal: <em>Do you know when she’ll be back?</em></li>\n<li><em>Could you take a message for him?</em> / <em>I’d like to leave her a message.</em> / <em>Please tell Padmini that Meera called.</em> / <em>Please ask her to call me back.</em> / <em>She can reach me at 7708645321.</em> / <em>Please tell him that I’m in town.</em> / <em>Please let her know that I would like to meet her.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Leaving a message (the lecture’s recorded call at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Meera calls; Padmini’s sister answers.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Meera:</strong> Hello, could I speak to Padmini, please? <strong>Sister:</strong> I’m sorry, she’s not around.</li>\n<li><strong>Meera:</strong> Oh, that’s all right. I would like to leave her a message. Could you please take a message for her? <strong>Sister:</strong> Yes, of course.</li>\n<li><strong>Meera:</strong> Please tell Padmini that Meera had called. You could also ask her to call me back. <strong>Sister:</strong> Sure, I will tell her.</li>\n<li><strong>Meera:</strong> She can reach me at 7708-645-321. <strong>Sister:</strong> Oh, wait a second, please. Let me grab a pen and a paper to make a note of that number. Could you please repeat your number?</li>\n<li><strong>Meera:</strong> Yeah, sure. My number is seven seven zero eight, six four five, three two one. <strong>Sister:</strong> Okay, fine, noted. <strong>Meera:</strong> Okay, thank you. Bye.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> ask for the person, leave a short message and your number, and repeat the number in small groups when asked (Lecture 4.3).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"taking-a-call\">Taking a call</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Answering, and asking who is calling</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=615s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:15</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Good morning / Good afternoon. (Company name), (your name) speaking.</em> For example: <em>Good afternoon. Airvoice Services. Megha speaking.</em></li>\n<li><em>Thank you for calling (company name). This is (your name).</em> For example: <em>Thank you for calling Airvoice Services. This is Megha. What can I do for you today?</em></li>\n<li><em>How may I help you? / What can I do for you?</em></li>\n<li>A voice you do not know: <em>Could I ask who’s calling?</em> / <em>May I ask who’s calling?</em> / <em>Who’s calling, please?</em> / <em>Who am I speaking to, please?</em> / <em>Where are you calling from, please?</em></li>\n<li>A voice you know: <em>Hello… is that Padmini?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A customer’s call (the lecture’s recorded call at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Agent:</strong> Good afternoon. Thank you for calling Airvoice Services. How may I help you?</li>\n<li><strong>Kavitha</strong> gives her name and area, and explains: <em>I had recharged my phone with an international roaming plan, which I see is still not activated.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Agent:</strong> Okay. Please hold on, ma’am. I’ll put you through to the technical team.</li>\n<li><strong>Agent</strong>, later: Hello, ma’am. It was a technical glitch from our side. Sorry for the inconvenience caused by the delay. The plan will be activated in 15 minutes. Anything else that I can help you with?</li>\n<li><strong>Kavitha:</strong> No, nothing else. Thank you so much. <strong>Agent:</strong> Thank you. Have a nice day.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> greet with the company’s name and offer help; ask the caller to wait (<em>hold on</em>) while you connect her (<em>put you through</em> = connect a caller to another person or team); apologise for the <em>glitch</em> (a small technical fault); close politely.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"asking-to-wait\">Asking to wait</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Asking someone to wait</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=666s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:06</a>). To <strong>hold on</strong> or <strong>hang on</strong> is to wait on the line.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To make the caller wait</th>\n<th>When you are busy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Could you hang on?</em></td>\n<td><em>I am tied up at the moment. Can I call you back in an hour’s time?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Could you hold on?</em></td>\n<td><em>If it is not urgent, can I buzz you later in the evening?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Would you mind holding, please?</em></td>\n<td><em>You just have two minutes; be quick in what you have to say.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hold on, let me grab a pen and a paper.</em></td>\n<td><em>I won’t be able to have a long chat as I am driving now.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Sorry to keep you waiting.</em></td>\n<td><em>This conversation will take longer; can I call you later?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Sorry, he is on the other line. Could I take a message?</em></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The caller, in turn, can ask: <em>Could I leave a message?</em> (<em>Tied up</em> = busy. To <em>buzz</em> someone = to call them, informally. <em>On the other line</em> = busy with another call.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two short calls (the lecture’s recorded calls at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A caller asks for Meera. <em>Could you hold on? I will check if she’s around.</em> Then: <em>Sorry to keep you waiting. Meera has gone to the nearby temple with her grandmother. She is not around. Do you want me to take a message for Meera?</em> The caller: <em>Oh, no thanks. I will buzz her tomorrow morning.</em></li>\n<li>Ananya, Nithya’s sister, calls for advice about the new BSc course launched by IIT Madras: <em>Is it a good time to talk?</em> The reply: <em>Ananya, I am tied up at the moment. Can I call you back in an hour’s time?</em> Ananya: <em>Sure, please call me at your best convenient time.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> ask the caller to hold, apologise for the wait, and offer to take a message. When you are busy, say so politely and offer to call back.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-second-call-and-a-conference-call\">A second call and a conference call</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">On receiving a second call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=792s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:12</a>), when another call comes in while you are talking:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I am receiving a second call. Could you hold for a second / minute?</em></li>\n<li><em>I am speaking to someone on the other side. If it is not urgent, can I call you in some time?</em></li>\n<li><em>I am getting a call from my professor. I will have to answer. I will call you back soon.</em></li>\n<li><em>Please hold on a sec. My boss is pinging me.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>To <em>ping</em> here is “a synonym for call”: <em>My boss is pinging me</em> = my boss is calling me (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conference call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>conference call</strong> is a call with several people at the same time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). The slide’s situation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=867s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:27</a>): Ram is talking to Geetha when Sita calls, and all three need to discuss office work.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ram to Geetha: <em>Sita is calling me</em> (or <em>pinging me</em>). <em>Can I get Sita with us?</em> Geetha: <em>Of course.</em></li>\n<li><em>Wait a second, let me rope in Sita into this call.</em> (To <strong>rope in</strong> = to bring someone in. Standard English uses one of the two: <em>let me rope Sita into this call</em>, or <em>let me rope in Sita</em>.)</li>\n<li>Ram to Sita: <em>I am having Geetha with me on the other side, I am patching you in.</em> (To <strong>patch in</strong> = to add someone to the call. Standard English also says simply <em>I have Geetha with me</em>.)</li>\n<li><em>Geetha and Sita, am I audible to both of you?</em> (<em>Audible</em> = can be heard.)</li>\n<li>At the end: <em>Geetha, please stay on the line. Sita, you could disconnect. I will call you later.</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"phone-problems\">Phone problems</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Is the other person still there?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s Situation 1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1014s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:54</a>): Geeta cannot hear the other person and is not sure they are still on the line.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Hello, are you there?</em></li>\n<li><em>Hello. Can you hear me?</em></li>\n<li><em>Hello. Am I audible?</em></li>\n<li><em>Hello. Is my voice clear enough?</em></li>\n<li><em>Can you hear me loud and clear?</em></li>\n<li><em>Is my voice too low?</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">When your battery is dying</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Situation 2 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1086s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:06</a>): Lenna is talking to Anjali, and Anjali’s phone is running out of charge.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Please be quick. My battery is about to die.</em></li>\n<li><em>I might lose you soon, as I am on 1% battery.</em></li>\n<li><em>My battery is about to drain. Please text me.</em></li>\n<li><em>Can I call you after charging my phone?</em></li>\n<li><em>Let me grab a charger and call you back.</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">When you cannot hear properly</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Situation 3 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>): Geeta cannot hear the other person well.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Say</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, I can hear you on and off.</em></td>\n<td>the sound comes and goes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, your voice is breaking.</em></td>\n<td>the voice is broken into pieces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, your voice is jarring.</em></td>\n<td>you hear a harsh, vibrating noise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, I can hear you, but in bits and pieces.</em></td>\n<td>you hear only parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, your voice is echoing.</em></td>\n<td>the voice comes back again, even your own: <em>Let me cut the call and call you back again</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Hello, I think you got cut off.</em></td>\n<td>the call has dropped</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Line problems and the wrong number</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1248s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:48</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a beep: the call does not connect</td>\n<td><em>I can’t get through.</em> / <em>I couldn’t get through to that number.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>several beeps: the person is on another call</td>\n<td><em>Looks like the line is busy. I’ll call her later.</em> / <em>The line is engaged.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no sound at all</td>\n<td><em>Looks like the phone is dead.</em> / <em>The line is unobtainable.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a caller asks for someone not at your number</td>\n<td><em>Sorry, I think you have got the wrong number.</em> / <em>There is none of that name here.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not just “Wrong number”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>We often just say <em>Wrong number</em>. Say instead: <em>Sorry, I think you have got the wrong number</em>, or <em>There is no one of that name here</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"ending-a-call\">Ending a call</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Before ending a call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSwUcHGAMcE&amp;t=1326s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:06</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>formal: the person you wanted is not there</td>\n<td><em>No problem. I will try again later.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>formal: you took a message for someone</td>\n<td><em>Okay, I will pass on the message.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>informal</td>\n<td><em>It was a great conversation. Let us connect regularly.</em> / <em>Take care. Let us be in touch.</em> / <em>Take care. Goodbye.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you must stop suddenly</td>\n<td><em>I might lose you soon. There is some connectivity issue here.</em> / <em>I need to hang up now. Let us continue the conversation tomorrow.</em> / <em>It’s been an hour we are talking. I need to get back to work.</em> / <em>Listen. My class is about to start. I will talk to you later.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(To <strong>hang up</strong> = to end the call. <em>It’s been an hour we are talking</em> is not standard English; say <em>We have been talking for an hour</em>, or <em>It’s been an hour since we started talking</em>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The phone words in one place</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>make a call / take a call</td>\n<td>call someone / answer a call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pick up</td>\n<td>answer the phone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hold on, hang on, hold</td>\n<td>wait on the line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>put someone through</td>\n<td>connect them to another person or team</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>on the other line</td>\n<td>busy with another call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tied up</td>\n<td>busy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>buzz, ping</td>\n<td>call (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rope in, patch in</td>\n<td>bring someone into a call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stay on the line</td>\n<td>do not end the call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>disconnect, cut the call, hang up</td>\n<td>end the call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get through</td>\n<td>manage to connect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cut off</td>\n<td>the call dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>busy, engaged</td>\n<td>the line is in use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dead, unobtainable</td>\n<td>no connection at all</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Preliminaries</strong> (4.1): English is the world’s language, in many accents; aim at the global standard. Something to say → politeness → slow tempo. Politeness in English: <em>please, thanks, excuse me, sorry, pardon me</em>; requests as questions (<em>Will you tell me the time, please?</em>); one <em>you</em> for all. Word stress varies (LON-don, ber-LIN, ja-PAN, U-nion, u-NI-ted). Vowels are very long (<em>car, boat</em>) or very short (<em>about, before</em>). Good speaking comes from good listening: the manner, not only the matter. Advanced learner’s dictionary; drill 10 minutes morning and evening.</p>\n<p><strong>Speak slowly</strong> (4.2): a slow tempo is clear, polite, and gives time to think. Pause is the punctuation of speech: <em>A woman, without her, man is nothing.</em> Rapid speech merges, drops and changes sounds. Self-test: count words, record, time. Rapid: over 3 or 4 syllables a second, or over 100 words a minute; pilots must stay under 100. Practise in your mother tongue too, then switch tempo for formal talk.</p>\n<p><strong>Phrasal pause</strong> (4.3): a phrase is words working as one unit. Pause after every phrase, clause and sentence (# and ##), with phrase &lt; clause &lt; sentence. Pause after greetings and identities, words of address, every 2 or 3 digits, each level of an address, discourse markers (<em>so, but, therefore, thus, of course, indeed, by all means</em>), each item in a series, and every noun, verb, adjectival and adverbial phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to say</strong> (4.4): we speak for countless purposes; great speakers are remembered for content. Two-minute civic talk: where, what, how much, its effects (at least the problem, the place, the extent, the harm). Quantify; find data (the Net, libraries and archives, folklore, elders). Interesting = good news, a good word, entertainment; relevant = no “flying to fish”. Photographs, maps, dictionary and thesaurus. Rehearse within the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Presentations</strong> (4.5): all conversations are presentations. Well turned out, appropriately dressed; relaxed but alert (people see more than they listen); greet with a smile; meaningful hands; slow-panning eye contact; a modulated voice; few bullets and few words per line, charts for numbers, colours with contrast. Creativity + language + data.</p>\n<p><strong>Telephone</strong> (4.6): make or take a call, formal or informal. Start (<em>This is Megha from …</em>), ask for someone (<em>May I speak to …, please?</em>), the purpose (<em>I’m calling to ask about …</em>), availability, a message, who is calling, waiting (<em>hold on, hang on</em>), a second call, a conference call (<em>rope in, patch in</em>), problems (<em>breaking, echoing, cut off, engaged, unobtainable, wrong number</em>), ending (<em>I’ll try again later</em>, <em>I need to hang up now</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make these requests polite in the English way: (a) <em>Tell me the way to the station.</em> (b) <em>Close the window.</em> (The sentences are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Lecture 4.1 gives two tools: turn the command into a <strong>question</strong>, and add a polite word such as <strong>please</strong> or <strong>excuse me</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <em>Will you please tell me the way to the station?</em> or <em>Excuse me, will you tell me the way to the station, please?</em></li>\n<li>(b) <em>Will you please close the window?</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A bare command (<em>Tell me…</em>, <em>Close…</em>) sounds rude in English, even with a smile; only army commanders give orders that way.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Mark the pauses (# short, ## long) and give the reason for each: <em>Good morning Professor I have brought the books the notes and the charts but the slides are not ready.</em> (The sentence is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Good morning # Professor ## I have brought the books # the notes # and the charts # but # the slides are not ready ##</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Good morning #</em>: a greeting.</li>\n<li><em>Professor ##</em>: a word of address, at the end of the first sentence.</li>\n<li><em>the books # the notes # and the charts #</em>: items in a series.</li>\n<li><em>but #</em>: a discourse marker (a contrast is coming), as in <em>Ruth was tired # but # not bored</em>.</li>\n<li><em>the slides are not ready ##</em>: the end of the sentence, the longest pause.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How do you say the phone number 9876501234 and the postal code 500032 so that the listener can write them down? (The numbers are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Lecture 4.3: pause after every two or three digits, and say each digit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In pairs: <em>98 # 76 # 50 # 12 # 34</em>, said <em>nine eight # seven six # five zero # one two # three four</em>.</li>\n<li>Or in threes, then twos: <em>987 # 650 # 12 # 34</em>.</li>\n<li>The postal code in two groups of three: <em>500 # 032</em>, said <em>five zero zero # zero three two</em>, like the lecture’s <em>Chennai 600 # 036</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Grouped like this, the listener does not need to ask you to repeat.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">You read a 200-word paragraph aloud, and the recorder shows 1 minute 20 seconds. Are you a rapid speaker by the lecture’s rule? (The numbers are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Time in seconds: 60 + 20 = 80 seconds.</li>\n<li>Words per second: 200 ÷ 80 = 2.5.</li>\n<li>Words per minute: 2.5 × 60 = 150.</li>\n<li>Compare with the limit: 150 is more than 100.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> yes. 150 words a minute is faster than the lecture’s 100 words a minute. To read the paragraph at 100 words a minute you need 200 ÷ 100 = 2 minutes, so slow down and pause after every group of words.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which phrase fits? (a) You answer the phone at a company called Bluebird Travels. (b) The caller’s voice keeps coming and going. (c) You must add a colleague, Ravi, to a call in progress. (d) A caller asks for “Mr Rao”, and no Mr Rao is at your number. (The situations are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <em>Good morning. Bluebird Travels. (Your name) speaking. How may I help you?</em> A formal answer: greeting, company, your name, an offer of help.</li>\n<li>(b) <em>Hello, I can hear you on and off</em>, or <em>Your voice is breaking</em> (Situation 3).</li>\n<li>(c) <em>Wait a second, let me rope Ravi into this call</em>; then to Ravi, <em>I am patching you in</em>; then <em>Am I audible to both of you?</em> (a conference call).</li>\n<li>(d) <em>Sorry, I think you have got the wrong number</em>, or <em>There is no one of that name here</em>, not just “Wrong number”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">You have two minutes to speak on “Garbage on the streets of my area”. Plan the talk with Lecture 4.4’s checklist. (The topic is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Name of the problem</strong> and <strong>name of the place</strong>: garbage left on your street, in your area; show a map if you use slides.</li>\n<li><strong>Extent (how much)</strong>: find the numbers; do not guess. How many bins, how often the garbage is collected, how many days it lies there. Ask the local office, residents and elders, and look for reports on the Net.</li>\n<li><strong>Harmful impact</strong>: smell, flies and mosquitoes, disease, blocked drains. Quantify where you can.</li>\n<li><strong>Data people can see</strong>: a photograph says it in one look.</li>\n<li><strong>Interesting and relevant</strong>: show how it hurts your listeners and what it costs them.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehearse with a timer</strong>: if you run over two minutes, cut details, but keep the four points the lecture says you must mention.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n"});
